[sam@math.ucla.edu (Sam Needham) writes:]
[] I like this with reservations. A monster on the diagonal from the player
[] will be on the same colour space every turn and so be 50/50 naughty
[] and nice. However a monster walking towards the player along a rectangular
[] direction, and starting out mean, will stay mean and head to the player
[] until it hits - because it will move one closer, change square colour, and
[] stay mean.

no, i patched my original conception of this idea to read that only
monsters adjacent to you are affected.  thus monsters heading towards
you react as always until they get to you.

[] Secondly, a smart player will be able to predict who's going to attack
[] him or her this round and take steps to avoid the most dangerous.

no, in the patch i suggested that the "influence" be determined by
the parity of the monster moves which the player can only determine
after the first monster hits.  this is slightly different than the
effect you may notice with boots of speed, for example.  in this way
every new encounter would start out sort of randomly.

btw, your moves and monster moves are kept in two different counters.

[] How about stacking - two rings of conflict?

interesting idea, i've proposed ring stacking schemes before and i
like the twist this would introduce: two rings of conflict would
simply force all the adjacent monsters to fight each other.

[] How about a +2 ring of conflict giving a base 70% chance instead of 50%?

hmm, ...

